editorial:
## Method and reasoning
Any optimum either stays inside the array or wraps around. A wrapping segment is the complement of a removed nonempty contiguous segment, giving total minus the minimum segment sum. If the ordinary maximum is negative, all elements are negative and the complement of the whole array would be empty. Return the ordinary maximum in that case.

## Walk through the sample
In [5, −8, 2, 3, 4], removing the interior minimum [−8] leaves the circular run 2, 3, 4, 5 with sum 14. On [−5, −2], removing the whole array would give an illegal empty run. The answer there is −2.

## Complexity
Time O(n), space O(1).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    long long top = LLONG_MIN, bottom = LLONG_MAX, total = 0, hi = 0, lo = 0;
    while (n--) {
        long long x;
        cin >> x;
        total += x;
        hi = max(x, hi + x);
        lo = min(x, lo + x);
        top = max(top, hi);
        bottom = min(bottom, lo);
    }
    cout << (top < 0 ? top : max(top, total - bottom)) << '\n';
}
~~~
